      "id": "rdls_exp-ken_rcmrd_housingstructurecement",
      "title": "Housing Structure_Cement",
      "description": "Housing materials are part of the Population and Housing indicators in DHS, used to assess household living standards, wealth index composition, and environmental health conditions.The DHS records materials for floors, walls, and roofs. Housing Structure made of Cement falls under the third category based on durability and quality: Finished (Improved) Materials These materials are durable, processed, and offer protection from the elements. Floor: Cement, polished wood, vinyl, tiles, marble, carpet Wall: Burnt bricks, cement blocks, concrete, stone Roof: Iron sheets, tiles, concrete, asbestos sheets Denotes improved housing quality, characteristic of higher wealth quintiles. Reference Frameworks DHS-8 (2023): Population and Housing Indicators - Household Electricity, Flooring Material, and Sleeping Rooms UN-Habitat (2020): Core Housing and Settlement Indicators Framework WHO/UNICEF JMP (2022): WASH and housing infrastructure standards. [Source: This metadata record was automatically extracted from the GeoNode portal rcmrd]",
